WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.960
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World is a decade old, and&nbsp;
this ode to the aimlessness of young adulthood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.880
still has a cult following. But&nbsp;
while the film is whimsical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.920
it also has its flaws. Here are aspects of&nbsp;
the film that take on new meaning for adults.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.440
The very first line of the movie informs us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.640
that 22-year-old Scott Pilgrim is dating a&nbsp;
high-schooler, the 17-year-old Knives Chau.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.880
"How old are you now Scott, like 28?"

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.400
Even though the audience subsequently&nbsp;
learns that they haven't even kissed yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.480
it's still icky, and only gets worse as you get&nbsp;
further and further from high school yourself.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.800
"You're too good for him. Run."

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.480
Some of Scott's friends, to their credit, point&nbsp;
out that Scott is hiding in a safe, pressure-free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.920
non-relationship in the wake of a bad breakup the&nbsp;
previous year. Others, however, are completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.280
fine with the idea, as Knives begins to attend&nbsp;
Scott's band practice and shows. His roommate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.760
Wallace for some reason agrees to wait outside&nbsp;
Knives' school with Scott until the final bell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:11.680
rings. At the film's end, Ramona even hints that&nbsp;
Scott and Knives should get back together. What?

00:01:11.849 --> 00:01:13.049
"You two make a good combo."

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.040
In fact, the ending originally shot for the&nbsp;
film had Scott and Knives getting back together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.240
When that divided test audiences, it was revised&nbsp;
to Scott and Ramona starting over instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.320
But speaking with Collider in 2021,&nbsp;
Edgar Wright , the film's director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.200
explained that even in making that adjustment,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.840
"The key to it was that Knives lets him go."

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.840
"I'm too cool for you anyway."

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:47.280
So even now, the one-sided relationship that many&nbsp;
would say has all the hallmarks of an adult man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.440
"grooming" a teenager is considered a legitimate&nbsp;
side of a love triangle? As the years go by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.680
that part of the movie gets harder to defend.
In a 2007 essay for the AV Club about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.160
movie Elizabethtown, pop-culture critic&nbsp;
Nathan Rabin coined a new phrase, saying,

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:10.320
"The Manic Pixie Dream Girl exists solely in the&nbsp;
fevered imaginations of sensitive writer-directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.640
to teach broodingly soulful young men to embrace&nbsp;
life and its infinite mysteries and adventures."

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.400
Within a few years, it became a widely-used term&nbsp;
to describe the many movies where a colorful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.800
lively young woman — like Kate Winslet&nbsp;
in Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.440
or Natalie Portman in Garden State — lures&nbsp;
a mopey male protagonist out of his shell.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.520
I-I make a noise or I do something&nbsp;
that no one has ever done before and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.560
then I can feel unique again, even&nbsp;
if it's only for like a second."

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.320
It's intriguing to consider in retrospect&nbsp;
whether Ramona Flowers conforms to this trope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.120
While she's a more detached, quiet character&nbsp;
than your bubbly Zooey Deschanel types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.400
she unfortunately fits all the other stereotypes.&nbsp;
She never appears in the movie on her own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.640
what glimpses we do get of her life are&nbsp;
only in regards to her past relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.560
and the movie never explains what&nbsp;
drew her to Scott in the first place.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.600
So, in place of personality and character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.120
the audience gets a female lead who has lots&nbsp;
of exes and colorful hair — along with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.520
montage of people at a party telling&nbsp;
Scott cryptic things about Ramona.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.160
"She's got some battle scars, dude!"

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.360
There is a relatively fun self-aware gag in&nbsp;
the movie about Ramona appearing in Scott's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.720
dreams via a convenient subspace highway, which&nbsp;
is how she becomes the girl of his dreams. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.200
although Mary Elizabeth Winstead brings&nbsp;
admirable depth to the role with her performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.640
as the years go by it becomes harder to not wish&nbsp;
there was more of a sense of who Ramona is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.640
she's not simply servicing Scott's story.
The way the LGBTQ characters are presented in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.240
Pilgrim might set off different reactions in&nbsp;
focus groups today than it did a decade ago.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.440
Wallace Wells, Scott's gay roommate, has an&nbsp;
active, seemingly polyamorous lifestyle. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.840
is fine — even Scott doesn't mind&nbsp;
it, despite sharing the same bed.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.200
"Hey, morning."

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:01.760
"Hey Jimmy"

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.120
"Double standard!"

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.480
But it seems like Wallace can't have a single&nbsp;
conversation without asking if boys go to Knives'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.600
school, or pretending that the girl Scott is&nbsp;
referring to is a boy. From stealing Scott's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.880
sister's boyfriend during a concert to watching&nbsp;
Lucas Lee film a movie because he's his crush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.560
it really is bizarre how every opportunity&nbsp;
Wallace has to speak turns into an opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.160
to reinforce his sexual identity. Keiran Culkin's&nbsp;
performance makes Wallace consistently amusing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.840
but revisit the movie again and you'll be&nbsp;
amazed at how one-note the character feels.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.040
Roxy Richter, meanwhile, is&nbsp;
all but ridiculed as a phase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.560
by Ramona when she's revealed&nbsp;
to be her fourth "Evil Ex."

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.760
Given no backstory or flashback other than&nbsp;
Ramona saying she was once bi-curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.520
Roxy seems to exist just to&nbsp;
make some lesbian-related puns.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.355
"Well honey, I'm a little bi-furious!"

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.280
Another strange aspect of Scott Pilgrim vs.&nbsp;
The World you might notice as you get older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:11.280
and more worldly is its off-kilter relationship&nbsp;
with non-white characters. Matthew Patel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.200
the first of Ramona's Evil Exes,&nbsp;
is of Indian-Canadian heritage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.720
a fact that isn't terribly relevant until he&nbsp;
launches into a Bollywood dance number near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.760
the end of his fight with Scott. The fifth&nbsp;
and sixth evil exes, the Katayanagi twins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.920
fall into the "silent Asian" stereotype that was&nbsp;
called out last year by Mulan actor Jimmy Wong.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:38.320
Even Knives, while she gets plenty of lines and&nbsp;
time to shine as a character beyond her ethnicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.040
is referred to in a strange, loaded&nbsp;
moment as Scott is talking to his friends.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.600
"Knives Chau. She's Chinese."

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.640
"Wicked."

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:56.240
It's a small moment, but it plays into the larger&nbsp;
trope of fetishizing Asian female characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.520
one that has come under fire in the&nbsp;
years since the film's release.
 
 &nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:06.000
Scott Pilgrim is unemployed. He's also 22&nbsp;
and dating a high schooler in a strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.960
sexless pretend-relationship. He shares&nbsp;
a studio apartment with another man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:17.280
who owns all of the furniture and the lone bed.&nbsp;
This studio apartment is across the street from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.320
the house where Scott grew up. Other than&nbsp;
perhaps playing bass in an unheralded band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.960
there isn't much about Scott&nbsp;
Pilgrim that doesn't scream "loser."

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.200
Scott spends nearly the entirety&nbsp;
of the film's runtime acting whiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.040
insecure, and selfish. He cheats&nbsp;
on his high school girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:41.280
then dumps her without explanation. He makes the&nbsp;
barest attempts at apologies and introspection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.720
right before the film is over, but it's clear&nbsp;
he hasn't grown much by the movie's conclusion.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.960
"Hey, hey, mind if I tag along?"

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.880
If they were to make a sequel, we'd most likely&nbsp;
discover ten years later that Scott is still not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.080
nearly as cool as the original&nbsp;
film frequently made him seem.
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.760
Chris Evans has appeared as Captain America&nbsp;
in the Marvel Universe in 11 different movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.720
It's hard not to imagine what comedic roles&nbsp;
he turned down to spend a decade in spandex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.000
after re-watching his uproarious turn as&nbsp;
Lucas Lee in Scott Pilgrim vs. The World.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.640
Beginning with the moment he cracks his neck&nbsp;
in time with the Universal Pictures fanfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:26.480
Evans generates the most consistent laughs per&nbsp;
minute of any of the Evil Exes, or perhaps any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:32.640
character in the film. Playing an absurdly aggro&nbsp;
movie star in a bit of self-parody, after his two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.800
movie turns as a pre-MCU Human Torch in the Fox&nbsp;
Fantastic Four movies, Evans has perfect timing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.800
in declaring that Scott Pilgrim is no competition&nbsp;
and throwing him into Toronto's iconic Casa Loma.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.360
"Hi. Big fan."

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:50.640
"Why wouldn't you be?"

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.560
With Captain America now behind&nbsp;
him, Evans' sly turn in Knives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.960
Out is hopefully just the tip of more&nbsp;
interesting, diverse roles to come.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.560
Beyond whether she qualifies as a&nbsp;
"Manic Pixie Dream Girl" cliche or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.200
Ramona Flowers has a frustrating lack of&nbsp;
agency in Scott Pilgrim vs. The World.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.840
This feels all the more grating when&nbsp;
you grow up and realize how complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.000
a good love story can be in movies&nbsp;
disciplined enough to properly develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.200
one. It's entirely unexplained why Ramona invites&nbsp;
Scott back to her place and makes out with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:28.480
on a "date" that was explicitly pitched as&nbsp;
a platonic hangout. While the revelation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:33.920
the League of Evil Exes complicates her story and&nbsp;
explains some of her detached attitude, it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.600
terribly clear how the League haunting her makes&nbsp;
her feel, other than as it pertains to Scott.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:45.280
When she abruptly leaves Scott to return to her&nbsp;
ex Gideon, it's a bummer but at least a decisive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:51.120
action that hints at deeper motivations. But even&nbsp;
that turns out to be telling someone else's story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.160
as Gideon has attached a mind-control device to&nbsp;
the back of her head, transforming her entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.920
into a damsel in distress for the film's big&nbsp;
action climax. Adding insult to injury, she mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.720
takes a backseat in the showdown with Gideon,&nbsp;
as Scott and Knives do most of the fighting.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.320
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World was celebrated&nbsp;
upon its release for having an array of diverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.680
female roles. But in actuality, it fails&nbsp;
the standard-bearer test of representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.080
in cinema known as "The Bechdel test."&nbsp;
Named for cartoonist Allison Bechdel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.080
it goes like this: To pass the&nbsp;
test, a film must have at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.640
two female characters that talk to one&nbsp;
another about something other than a man.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.240
Revisiting Scott Pilgrim vs. The World, it&nbsp;
never quite passes. Even when Ramona, Knives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.760
Envy or others make small talk with another&nbsp;
female character, it's always about Scott, Gideon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:48.400
or some other mutual male friend or ex-boyfriend.&nbsp;
The Bechdel test sounds so simple — give your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.120
character enough depth to say something other&nbsp;
than a line that advances the male antagonist's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.880
story! — but in films like Pilgrim, it is&nbsp;
apparently impossible to imagine these female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:05.280
characters having their own distinct lives.
As adults, it's easy to be hard on an unemployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.720
loser like Scott Pilgrim. As it's a coming-of-age&nbsp;
story, you might say he's supposed to be a loser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:16.560
in the beginning. Surely he grows and changes&nbsp;
over the course of the film? In a word, no.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.640
Scott spends the majority of the&nbsp;
film as a self-centered, entitled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.880
whiny and defensive character. When he&nbsp;
reluctantly admits to Knives and Ramona&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.760
that he cheated on both of them, he at&nbsp;
last has an opportunity to act mature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.600
but Gideon stabs him in the back,&nbsp;
killing him. In a video-game twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:38.960
an extra life that Scott earned earlier allows&nbsp;
him to come back and replay the final fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:44.240
again. This time, he gets a brief monologue&nbsp;
where he reveals to Knives and Ramona that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:50.080
cheated on them voluntarily, but still doesn't&nbsp;
have to deal with any significant consequences.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:52.320
"So are we all good?"

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.200
"Never felt better."

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:02.720
In this final sequence, the first time through,&nbsp;
he fights Gideon with a sword he earned through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:09.040
the power of love, but on the second pass he earns&nbsp;
it through the power of self-respect. This makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:15.440
no sense, because Scott has demonstrated that&nbsp;
if anything, he has too much self-respect. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:21.040
there a sword you earn by respecting the women in&nbsp;
your life? Because Scott needs to earn that one.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:25.760
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World is&nbsp;
largely a video game-inspired fantasy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:31.840
so maybe it's not fair to hold it to a coherent&nbsp;
standard in terms of today's gender politics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.720
But the older you get, the more you start to&nbsp;
realize that the League of Evil Exes is more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:43.520
just a framework to structure the story around&nbsp;
— it's toxic masculinity in its purest form.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:48.000
Without much nuance or backstory, the&nbsp;
fighting in the movie reduces dating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:53.440
to a tournament where Ramona is the prize,&nbsp;
objectifying women as objects to be fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.080
over instead of human beings with&nbsp;
willpower, thoughts and feelings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:03.680
While this is partially the result of distilling&nbsp;
a six-volume graphic novel into a two hour film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:09.360
it ultimately creates something that's fun and&nbsp;
flashy, but reduces gender roles to a digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:15.920
binary: men are stoic warriors, while women are&nbsp;
princesses in towers to be claimed as rewards.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.920
With the exception of Michael Cera, virtually&nbsp;
every member of the cast of Scott Pilgrim vs. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:27.920
World was more or less on-the-rise, and has seen&nbsp;
their careers blossom in the years since. Looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:32.640
back now, you see Aubrey Plaza before Parks and&nbsp;
Recreation, Chris Evans and Brie Larson before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:38.640
the MCU got them, Anna Kendrick before Up in&nbsp;
the Air and Pitch Perfect, and the list goes on.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:43.040
But when the film hit theaters in 2010,&nbsp;
the consensus seemed to be that Ellen Wong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:48.320
had stolen the show as Knives Chau and&nbsp;
seemed poised to be the film's breakout.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:52.160
In the years since, Wong has worked&nbsp;
steadily, playing a semi-prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:56.720
supporting role on Netflix's GLOW for&nbsp;
three years before its cancellation — yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:02.160
the Ellen-Wong-mania that seemed about to&nbsp;
materialize a decade ago never quite went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:07.680
down as planned. Looking back now as an adult,&nbsp;
perhaps it's time to once again appreciate her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:13.120
performance — and seek out some of the smaller&nbsp;
roles she's capably filled in the years since.

